    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-20524" textLabel="flavone synthase II" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q0JFI2" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">In dicots, genes encoding |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.162 EC 1.14.14.162, flavanone 2-hydroxylase| and |FRAME: EC-1.14.19.76 EC 1.14.19.76, flavone synthase II| are very similar. A BLASTP search of the rice proteome for sequences with closest homology to |FRAME: G-12187 CYP93G2|, which encodes a flavanone 2-hydroxylase, identifed the |FRAME: G-58314 CYP93G1| gene as a potential candidate for encoding |FRAME: EC-1.14.19.76 EC 1.14.19.76, flavone synthase II|. Like |FRAME: G-12187 CYP93G2|, with which it shares 60% identity, the |FRAME: G-58314 CYP93G1| gene encodes a cytochrome P450 protein containing diagnostic signatures of a Pro hinge region, an oxygen-binding pocket, and a heme-binding motif |CITS: [24843076]|. To characterize the protein, the coding region of the gene was expressed in the yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae, and microsomes prepared from recombinant yeast cells were incubated with flavanone substrates in the presence of NADPH. |FRAME: G-58314 CYP93G1|-expressing microsomes catalyzed the conversion of |FRAME: NARINGENIN-CMPD (2&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-naringenin| to |FRAME: CPD-431 apigenin| and |FRAME: CPD-6994 (2S)-eriodictyol| to |FRAME: 5734-TETRAHYDROXYFLAVONE luteolin| |CITS: [24843076]|. In addition, the coding sequence of |FRAME: G-58314 CYP93G1| was placed under the control of the cauliflower mosaic virus 35S promoter and transformed into the |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| transparent |FRAME: AT3G51240 F3H| mutant, which lacks |FRAME: EC-1.14.11.9 EC 1.14.11.9, flavanone 3-dioxygenase|. In addition, rice genes encoding |FRAME: EC-1.14.20.5 EC 1.14.20.5, flavone synthase I| were also transformed into the mutants. HPLC-MS/MS analysis detected the presence of the flavones |FRAME: CPD-431 apigenin|, |FRAME: 5734-TETRAHYDROXYFLAVONE luteolin|, and |FRAME: 574-TRIHYDROXY-3-METHOXYFLAVONE chrysoeriol| (which are not naturally present in Arabidopsis) only in the |FRAME: G-58314 CYP93G1| transgenic plants. The plants also contained different &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glycosides of the flavones |CITS: [24843076]|. A mutant rice line lacking the gene function was derived from a hemizygous insertion parent that harbors a T-DNA insertion in the second exon of the gene. The mutants lacked |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| and its &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glycosides, and instead showed significant increases (2- to 60-fold) in the accumulation of different flavone C-glycosides. Thus |FRAME: G-58314 CYP93G1| is essential for the biosynthesis of tricin-derived metabolites.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER-20524" />
